WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.959
♪♪♪ Upbeat synth horns ♪♪♪

00:00:00.959 --> 00:00:02.836
- [Kassidi Macak:] I think this team's legacy is huge.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.255
The Muslim girls basketball team that's dominating.

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:06.423
They're proud of their faith,

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:08.466
and they want it a part of their story.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.803
A lot of those girls probably don't think

00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:13.680
that they are representing something

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.056
bigger than themselves.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:16.766
They're high school kids.

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:18.435
How unfair is it for them to have to

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:20.311
take that responsibility

00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:22.689
and do it so well every single day.

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:25.942
They've totally shifted the community's

00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.528
and now even maybe a national idea

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.321
of what this team can be—

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:31.990
and an all-Muslim team can be.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:46.504
♪♪♪ Slow synth bells ♪♪♪

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:52.177
[Hallway chatter]

00:00:52.177 --> 00:01:03.730
♪♪♪ Slow synth bells ♪♪♪

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.773
- [Safiya Schaub:] I've been playing basketball

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:05.732
since seventh grade.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.234
My older brother played basketball,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.153
and I went to a lot of his games

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:11.821
so, um, my dad was like, you know,

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:13.198
"Why don't you try that?"

00:01:14.449 --> 00:01:15.617
- [Nadira Ali:] We go out there and we represent

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.910
more than just a basketball team.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.036
We represent our religion.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:19.871
We represent, uh, Salam school.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:21.372
We represent girls.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:23.833
- [Heba Badwan:] I feel like some people do

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:26.419
have negative image about us Muslims.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:27.837
They downgrade us even though

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:29.422
we're, like, normal people, you know?

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:30.882
We're out there like normal people.

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:32.467
We play like normal people.

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:34.969
- [Safiya:] Freshman year, we were kind of

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:36.429
a joke almost.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.722
The students would say,

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:40.058
"Why would we go to one of their games?"

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:42.310
We would never really have any support.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.395
- [Jumana Badwan:] We took that negativity

00:01:44.395 --> 00:01:45.563
to, like, positivity.

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:47.899
Our record right now—we're 12-4.

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:53.780
♪♪♪ Slow synth strings ♪♪♪

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.326
- [Abdullah Badwan:] The girls program's only been

00:01:58.326 --> 00:01:59.452
around for how long?

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:03.832
- [Abdullah:] Like, I think it's five, six years?
- [Jumana:] Yeah.

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:05.333
- [Abdullah:] Like, back in—back to

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:06.835
last year or two years ago, you guys

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:08.419
were just all over the place.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.256
But now, everybody's on the same page

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:12.465
and you guys are just rolling.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.299
- Yeah.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.759
- You gotta give props to the coach,

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:16.261
honestly, you know?

00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:18.555
Cuz they went through, like, a bunch of coaches,

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:20.682
and it just never worked out

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:22.100
and then she just came out of nowhere.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.854
♪♪♪ Pop keyboard ♪♪♪

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:28.064
- I came, like, the first day of practice, and I was like,

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:30.692
"Oh, my gosh, we need to practice every day.

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:32.569
I'm gonna schedule more games, more scrimmages.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:34.571
We just need to be around basketball more."

00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:38.074
I saw the talent the girls had, but it's just like,

00:02:38.074 --> 00:02:39.075
they weren't playing enough.

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:43.246
♪♪♪ Pop keyboard ♪♪♪

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.206
Every coach I've ever had since I was in third grade

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:46.416
has been a man,

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:48.543
so for me, a female coach and coaching

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:49.544
a female team,

00:02:49.544 --> 00:02:50.712
I thought it was pretty cool,

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:51.963
and I don't know how much

00:02:51.963 --> 00:02:53.923
the first two years that they even picked up on

00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:56.009
how different that is—to have a female coach.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:57.427
There's not many.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.682
♪♪♪ Pop keyboard ♪♪♪

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.017
- [Heba:] Our coach always tells us

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:05.435
it doesn't matter what the score is—

00:03:05.435 --> 00:03:07.312
as long as we play our hardest, like, we're good.

00:03:07.312 --> 00:03:10.440
We're always pushing for the best, you know?

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.357
- [Safiya:] She always says,

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.442
"Think of the controllables."

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:14.235
Sometimes the shots just don't fall in.

00:03:14.235 --> 00:03:15.570
So she always says,

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.697
"Play defense while you move your feet.

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.490
Focus on the controllables."

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.409
- Sometimes I don't even have to coach cuz

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:23.369
they know exactly what they should be doing.

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:27.498
[Crowd cheering]

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.585
- [Safiya:] I would definitely describe

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:32.503
how we play as aggressive.

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:34.088
Most people, you know, maybe

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:36.925
wouldn't think that we play aggressively,

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:38.760
but, yeah, definitely aggressive.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.677
- [Leo Schaub:] I think they're

00:03:39.677 --> 00:03:41.721
a little bit underestimated.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.265
And I would say that in general

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:45.892
of the Muslim teams that's

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.268
played over the times in Milwaukee.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.269
I think that other teams

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:49.270
are kind of surprised.

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:50.980
- [Safiya:] When they're warming up,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.232
you can tell, like, oh, they're, like,

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:54.025
looking at us while we're shooting or whatever, 

00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:56.110
and they're like, just joking around or they're

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.528
laughing or whatnot.

00:03:57.528 --> 00:03:59.197
Coach always tells us, too, she's like,

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.324
"Hey, look, they're not taking you seriously."

00:04:01.324 --> 00:04:03.576
It is like a secret weapon in a way.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.869
♪♪♪

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:07.705
- [Collective:] Stars on three. One! Two! Three! Stars!

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.291
♪♪♪

00:04:10.291 --> 00:04:11.459
- [Rula Sarsour:] Then when we go in,

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:13.503
we put all our effort in, and we come out,

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:15.463
then they're surprised, you know?

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:20.677
They think, "How do we play wearing a scarf?

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:23.471
I never really knew girls could play like this.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:24.764
Especially Muslims."

00:04:28.476 --> 00:04:34.691
[Crowd cheering]

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:49.789
♪♪♪ Mellow synth ♪♪♪

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.416
- [Zayna Tubeishat:] There are people who are, like,

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:53.001
against us, you know,

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:55.044
playing basketball.

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:02.427
- [Safiya:] We definitely get a lot of stares,

00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:03.678
and they're thinking, like, "Oh,

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:07.181
there's actually Muslim girls that play competitively?"

00:05:08.391 --> 00:05:10.560
- [Zayna:] People see just, like, a religion first

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.437
because obviously we're wearing the scarves.

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:14.772
- [Nadira:] There's always stereotypes

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:16.441
that are gonna be thrown around.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:18.943
They'll be like, 

00:05:18.943 --> 00:05:20.486
"Oh, they wear scarves cuz they're oppressed,"

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:21.404
and stuff like that.

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.866
- [Safiya:] I mean, in my family, where I grew up,

00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:26.242
it was always, like,

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:27.577
the same between, like—
- [Offscreen:] Equal.

00:05:27.577 --> 00:05:29.912
- [Safiya:] —equal between my brothers and me.

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:34.584
Honestly, I love wearing a scarf,

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:35.710
and I love my religion.

00:05:37.378 --> 00:05:39.297
I don't have a problem with

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:40.381
wearing it while I play,

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:43.176
and I don't feel uncomfortable. I don't feel hot.

00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:48.056
I think right now, like, it's good they say

00:05:48.056 --> 00:05:50.183
that we're a Muslim girls basketball team

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.602
to kind of create that normalization.

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:54.520
Or like, "Oh, look at these Muslims."

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.189
It's not an article about them

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:57.899
doing some wrongdoings or whatnot.

00:05:57.899 --> 00:05:59.233
Instead it's an article about

00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:01.069
them doing something great and empowering.

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:03.363
Maybe one day we'll come to a time

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:05.156
where that title will be dropped.

00:06:05.156 --> 00:06:07.283
- But, like, we don't let it get in the way.

00:06:07.283 --> 00:06:09.911
We actually, like, try to work harder just to

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:12.538
break those stereotypes and prove them wrong.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:17.752
♪♪♪ Driving beats ♪♪♪

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:20.213
[Crowd cheering]

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:21.547
- [Safiya:] As a senior,

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:23.591
I think about my relationship with my teammates

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:25.593
and how close we've gotten.

00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:28.846
Basketball is just, like, us.

00:06:28.846 --> 00:06:30.598
This is it.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:41.109
[Background voices]

00:06:41.109 --> 00:06:43.027
- [Kassidi:] Is anything spicy? Is this spicy?

00:06:43.444 --> 00:06:44.946
- [Anisa:] No, actually, I didn't make spicy.

00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:46.030
Well, for me, it's not spicy.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.157
I don't know how—how you guys…
[Laughing]

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.826
- [:] Well, she doesn't like anything spicy.

00:06:49.826 --> 00:06:50.743
- [Anisa:] Oh, OK.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:53.788
- [Kassidi:] I'm getting better. I'm getting better.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.456
- [Leo:] So I see the brackets came out.

00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:56.457
- [Kassidi:] Mmhm.

00:06:56.457 --> 00:06:57.834
- [Leo:] I'm kinda surprised that

00:06:57.834 --> 00:07:00.169
Salam School is rated 15th in the brackets.

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:01.462
- [Kassidi:] Yeah.

00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:02.964
Last Saturday,

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:03.965
I don't know if you guys know this,

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:06.926
but I go to a seeding meeting up in Sheboygan,

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:08.678
and all the coaches in that region

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:10.596
come in with their season summaries.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.014
You sit down and you vote

00:07:12.014 --> 00:07:13.850
where you think you should be seeded.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:16.853
When we went in, we were 11-4.

00:07:16.853 --> 00:07:18.896
Just scream out some numbers. Out of 15,

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:20.857
what seed do you think you guys should have been?

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.733
- [Offscreen:] At least like a 9.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:23.943
-At least like an 11?

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:24.610
- [Offscreen:] 5. 

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:25.528
10.
7.

00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:26.904
- A 10? 7?

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:29.323
I would agree. Somewhere in the middle, right?

00:07:29.323 --> 00:07:31.325
All the teams voted us last.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.286
- What?

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:34.871
- [Kassidi:] And I was like, "We're last?

00:07:34.871 --> 00:07:36.873
Are you kidding me? Why are you voting this way?

00:07:36.873 --> 00:07:39.542
Because I need to know. Because if it's strength of

00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:42.837
schedule, we beat teams that you have lost to.

00:07:42.837 --> 00:07:43.838
- We're rated last,

00:07:43.838 --> 00:07:46.007
but there are teams with one or three wins.

00:07:46.007 --> 00:07:46.883
- [Kassidi:] Yep.

00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:49.385
- And that was it? And we fell behind them?

00:07:49.385 --> 00:07:50.803
- [Kassidi:] We fell behind them.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.263
My opinion is, we've always been last.

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:53.598
They've always voted us last.

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:56.017
That's like a cowardly thing to do,

00:07:56.017 --> 00:07:57.894
where they don't have to worry about us.

00:07:58.853 --> 00:08:00.313
- [Abdullah:] Honestly, as the boys,

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:03.024
if we had a 14-4 season, which we don't,

00:08:03.024 --> 00:08:05.735
we would be, we would be, very, very, like,

00:08:05.735 --> 00:08:07.278
we'd probably be at least a third.

00:08:07.278 --> 00:08:09.697
I feel like it's just a bias towards them more.

00:08:09.697 --> 00:08:10.656
- Yeah, it is.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:11.991
- So we play the No. 2 seed

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:14.785
in the region on Tuesday, Catholic Central.

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:18.289
So don't let that unfairness dictate how you play.

00:08:18.289 --> 00:08:31.677
♪♪♪ Ethereal synth ♪♪♪

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:32.887
- [Kassidi:] Are you guys feeling a little bit of

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:34.514
added pressure than any other game?

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:35.348
- [Offscreen:] Yeah. 
- [Offscreen:] Yes.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:35.932
- [Offscreen:] Kind of.

00:08:35.932 --> 00:08:36.933
- [Kassidi:] They're the second seed.

00:08:36.933 --> 00:08:38.100
They're expected to win, right? 

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.227
We already know that.

00:08:40.144 --> 00:08:43.064
Come out strong, with intensity, and never let up.

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:48.069
♪♪♪ Ethereal synth with beats ♪♪♪

00:08:48.069 --> 00:08:50.112
[Crowd cheering]

00:08:50.112 --> 00:09:03.584
♪♪♪ Ethereal synth with beats ♪♪♪

00:09:03.584 --> 00:09:05.253
- [Offscreen:] Come on, guys. We got this!

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:06.546
- It's not about the score!

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:08.172
- [Collective:] It's more than the score!

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:10.508
- Don't give up ladies, do not give up.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.843
- [Nadira:] On the court, there's controllables:

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:15.471
You can control boxing out.

00:09:15.471 --> 00:09:17.181
You can control how well you play.

00:09:17.682 --> 00:09:19.433
You cannot control the other team.

00:09:19.433 --> 00:09:20.977
You cannot control the referees.

00:09:23.437 --> 00:09:24.855
And same thing in life:

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:26.399
You can control what you choose to do

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:27.692
in a certain situation,

00:09:30.361 --> 00:09:32.238
but you cannot control what other people think.

00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:34.740
You cannot control how they will look at you.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.243
It's just how life works.

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:50.715
- [Kassidi:] From my first year, you guys have come,

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:52.174
like, crazy far. 

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:53.259
I'm proud of you guys.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:54.302
The whole season you had. 

00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:55.845
And regardless of tonight's score,

00:09:55.845 --> 00:09:58.055
that doesn't reflect who you guys are as players.

00:09:58.055 --> 00:09:59.307
That team was supposed to be

00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:00.391
the No. 1 seed in the region,

00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:02.977
so, pretty monumental task to take down

00:10:02.977 --> 00:10:04.520
the No. 1 seed in the region, right?

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:05.062
- [Offscreen:] Yeah.

00:10:05.062 --> 00:10:06.063
- So you guys played hard, 

00:10:06.063 --> 00:10:07.064
and I'm really proud of you guys.

00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:08.858
- I'm proud of you too.

00:10:08.858 --> 00:10:09.859
- You're proud of me?

00:10:09.859 --> 00:10:11.402
I'm trying to create a culture here where

00:10:11.402 --> 00:10:13.404
the girls basketball team isn't a joke.

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:14.739
No one came to their games.

00:10:14.739 --> 00:10:15.740
They always lost.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.116
They didn't care about them.

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:18.409
And now with them winning,

00:10:18.409 --> 00:10:20.786
they wear their backpack tags, their sweatsuits.

00:10:20.786 --> 00:10:22.496
They have pride in their program.

00:10:22.496 --> 00:10:23.497
Everyone else in the school

00:10:23.497 --> 00:10:25.583
and the community also has that pride now.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:27.752
- [Safiya:] It's not just about, like,

00:10:27.752 --> 00:10:29.420
winning or the score.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.213
You know, if I get this many rebounds.

00:10:31.422 --> 00:10:32.298
- [Kassidi:] The biggest thing

00:10:32.298 --> 00:10:33.424
I want them to take off the court is

00:10:33.424 --> 00:10:34.884
being a confident woman.

00:10:35.217 --> 00:10:37.094
Don't let anyone tell you

00:10:37.094 --> 00:10:39.013
who you should be or how you should act.

00:10:39.013 --> 00:10:40.640
You be who you are

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.224
because you're confident in who you are.

00:10:43.017 --> 00:10:44.477
- [Safiya:] I am really proud 

00:10:44.477 --> 00:10:45.936
to go out and play competitively

00:10:45.936 --> 00:10:47.647
and be one of the only competitive

00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:50.149
Muslim girl teams in America playing.

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:51.150
I love you guys.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:51.942
- [Offscreen:] Love you too.

00:10:51.942 --> 00:10:52.526
- Love you too.

00:10:52.526 --> 00:10:53.027
- So.

00:10:53.027 --> 00:10:54.236
- [Kassidi:] All right. To more than the score.

00:10:54.236 --> 00:10:57.782
- [Collective:] To more than the score. Yay.

00:10:57.782 --> 00:11:09.877
♪♪♪ Synth strings ♪♪♪

00:11:10.461 --> 00:11:11.837
- [Kassidi:] I appreciate all the alumni coming back.

00:11:11.837 --> 00:11:12.588
You guys have definitely

00:11:12.588 --> 00:11:14.006
helped build a program here.

00:11:16.717 --> 00:11:18.052
I really appreciate you guys

00:11:18.052 --> 00:11:19.387
putting in the hard work and dedication

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:20.346
every year.

00:11:20.346 --> 00:11:21.472
So I hope you come back

00:11:21.472 --> 00:11:23.265
and show them what it means to be a Salam Star.

00:11:25.601 --> 00:11:28.938
So bring it in. Aaand...

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:30.606
- [Sue Bird:] Aight, what's the cheer?

00:11:30.606 --> 00:11:31.565
What are we saying?

00:11:31.565 --> 00:11:32.483
- Oh, my god!

00:11:32.483 --> 00:11:33.484
- Oh, my god!

00:11:33.484 --> 00:11:35.277
[Excited chatter and cheering]

00:11:35.277 --> 00:11:36.987
- [Collective:] One! Two! Three! Stars!

00:11:36.987 --> 00:11:38.739
[Laughing]

00:11:40.324 --> 00:11:42.535
- Hey, Sue, Rula can really shoot
- [Sue:] Yeah?

00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:43.369
so drop her a dime.

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:44.954
- Listen, that's what I do.

00:11:45.246 --> 00:11:46.831
- [Kassidi:] She's pretty tall. She's pretty athletic,

00:11:46.831 --> 00:11:48.874
so, uh, who's gonna match up with her?

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:49.834
- Not it.

00:11:49.834 --> 00:11:52.461
- Jenin? Jumana? Yeah! Jumana!

00:11:52.461 --> 00:11:53.754
- [Sue:] You guys ready?

00:11:53.754 --> 00:11:54.714
All right, let's tip it off.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:58.217
Ready? Here we go.

00:11:58.968 --> 00:12:02.346
[Cheering]

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:04.557
- I feel bad. I don't wanna take any of 'em out.

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:07.351
Who wants a sub? 

00:12:09.228 --> 00:12:11.021
You sure? OK.

00:12:12.022 --> 00:12:13.023
It sounds like one of the things

00:12:13.023 --> 00:12:14.024
we might have in common is,

00:12:14.024 --> 00:12:15.443
is some naysayers, right?

00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:16.318
That's something that,

00:12:16.318 --> 00:12:17.486
as a women's professional athlete,

00:12:17.486 --> 00:12:20.740
sadly, we face all the time.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:22.700
So whether it's the stupid comments on Twitter

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.368
telling us to stay in the kitchen

00:12:24.368 --> 00:12:26.078
or it's just people who put us down

00:12:26.078 --> 00:12:29.457
or try to compare us to our male counterpart.

00:12:29.457 --> 00:12:30.332
And it's tough.

00:12:30.332 --> 00:12:33.252
I mean, I think it's OK to feel bad about that—

00:12:33.252 --> 00:12:34.462
and then realize,

00:12:34.462 --> 00:12:37.006
"No. What somebody else thinks of me?

00:12:37.006 --> 00:12:38.007
Doesn't mean anything."

00:12:39.008 --> 00:12:42.845
[Cheering]

00:12:44.346 --> 00:12:46.015
Thank you guys so much.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:47.767
Just had a great time at the Salam School

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:48.934
at the alumni game.

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:49.685
I don't know about you guys,

00:12:49.685 --> 00:12:50.811
but I got buckets,

00:12:50.811 --> 00:12:53.022
and now I'm an official superfan.

00:12:53.022 --> 00:12:54.023
Thanks for having me.

